> The myrtle appears in only seven verses, yet a coherent pattern emerges: a low-growing, fragrant evergreen that replaces the cursed thorn order, constitutes the material of God's dwelling-booth, and shelters the place where God stands among His people. Esther's hidden Hebrew name — Hadassah, "myrtle-girl" — embodies the same profile: humble, concealed, faithful, fragrant under pressure.

---

## The Word

**H1918 הֲדַס (hădaç)** — "the myrtle (tree)." Of uncertain derivation, possibly a loanword. The KJV renders it "myrtle" or "myrtle tree(s)" in all six occurrences. YLT likewise uses "myrtle." The LXX generally translates with μυρσίνη (*myrsínē*), though in {{ref:Zec.1.8}} the LXX renders "myrtle trees" as ὄρη τὰ κατάσκια ("shady mountains") — interpreting rather than translating, apparently equating the myrtle grove with nations or kingdoms.

**H1919 הֲדַסָּה (Hădassâh)** — feminine form of H1918. Used once, in {{ref:Est.2.7}}, as Esther's Hebrew name before it is replaced by her Persian name.

**The plant.** The myrtle (*Myrtus communis*) is an evergreen shrub native to the Mediterranean, typically growing 2–5 meters — modest compared to the cedar, which towers above it. It produces white blossoms and dark berries. Its leaves are small, glossy, and aromatic; the fragrance intensifies when the leaves are crushed. It thrives in lowlands, valleys, and shaded areas rather than on exposed heights.

---

## Key Passages

With only seven occurrences, each verse is a key passage. They are examined here in canonical order, with the three Zechariah verses grouped together.

### Nehemiah 8:15 — Myrtle branches for Sukkot booths

{{ref:Neh.8.15}} lists myrtle branches alongside olive, palm, pine, and "thick trees" as the materials for building booths (H5521 *sukkah*) during the post-exile restoration of the Feast of Tabernacles. The text notes this observance had not been kept in this way "since the days of Joshua son of Nun" (v. 17), and the result was "very great gladness." Myrtle here serves as literal construction material for God's appointed temporary dwelling.

### Isaiah 41:19 — God plants myrtle in the wilderness

{{ref:Isa.41.19}} describes God planting seven types of trees in the wilderness and desert: cedar, shittah, myrtle, oil tree, fir, pine, and box. The myrtle appears among larger, more prominent species. The purpose clause follows in v. 20: "that they may see, and know, and consider, and understand together, that the hand of the LORD hath done this." The planting of trees in a barren place is presented as an act of divine transformation — wilderness becoming garden.

### Isaiah 55:13 — Myrtle replaces the brier

{{ref:Isa.55.13}} provides the most explicit symbolic framing: "Instead of the thorn shall come up the fir tree, and instead of the brier shall come up the myrtle tree: and it shall be to the LORD for a name, for an everlasting sign (H226) that shall not be cut off." The myrtle is positioned as the direct replacement for the brier — the anti-thorn. Given that thorns appear elsewhere as an image of fruitlessness and worldly choking (e.g., {{ref:Mat.13.22}}), this substitution carries weight. The resulting state is described as permanent: an "everlasting sign not cut off."

### Zechariah 1:8, 10, 11 — The man among the myrtle trees

These three verses belong to a single vision. In {{ref:Zec.1.8}}, a man on a red horse stands among the myrtle trees "in the bottom" (H4699 *mᵉtsullâh*). The Strong's definition of H4699 is "shade," derived from H6751 *tsâlal* ("to shadow, to darken"), though the KJV renders it "bottom" — suggesting a low-lying, shaded valley rather than an elevated or prominent position.

In {{ref:Zec.1.10}}, the man among the myrtle trees explains that the horsemen are agents the LORD sent "to walk to and fro through the earth." In {{ref:Zec.1.11}}, these agents report back to "the angel of the LORD that stood among the myrtle trees" — the LORD's representative inhabits the myrtle grove. Their report: "all the earth sitteth still, and is at rest." God's response (vv. 14–15) is jealousy for Jerusalem and anger at the nations who are "at ease."

The spatial picture is striking: the angel of the LORD stands in the shade, among the low myrtle trees, while the nations sit comfortably. The myrtle grove is the location of God's hidden presence, not a seat of visible power.

The LXX's decision to render "myrtle trees" as "shady mountains" is worth noting — it suggests the translators understood the myrtles as representing something larger (nations or kingdoms), though this is interpretation rather than translation.

### Esther 2:7 — Hadassah, the myrtle-girl

{{ref:Est.2.7}} introduces Esther with her Hebrew name first: "Hadassah, that is, Esther." The details are compact but resonant: orphaned (no father or mother), raised by her cousin Mordecai, "fair and beautiful." Her Hebrew name — Myrtle — is mentioned once and then replaced by her Persian name for the remainder of the book. She is a Jewish girl concealed within a foreign empire, hidden identity intact, who ultimately risks death to intercede for her people. Whether intentional or not, this profile aligns with the characteristics of the myrtle plant and the myrtle's position in the Zechariah vision: low, hidden, fragrant under pressure, faithful.

---

## Patterns Across Scripture

The following observations emerge from the seven occurrences. Given the small corpus, these patterns are suggestive rather than definitive.

1. **Anti-thorn.** {{ref:Isa.55.13}} explicitly positions the myrtle as the replacement for the brier. This is the only passage that directly contrasts myrtle with thorns, but its language is unambiguous: where the cursed plant grew, the myrtle now stands.

2. **Low and hidden position.** The myrtle is not a towering tree. In {{ref:Isa.41.19}} it appears alongside cedars but is physically modest by comparison. In {{ref:Zec.1.8}} the myrtle grove is located in the shade/bottom — a low, obscured position. Esther/Hadassah is hidden within a foreign court. The myrtle appears consistently in positions that are not prominent or powerful.

3. **Fragrance through crushing.** The myrtle plant's most distinctive physical property is that its fragrance releases when its leaves are crushed. This production chain — pressure producing something pleasing — parallels the olive-to-oil sequence (fruit pressed to yield oil). Scripture does not make this connection explicitly, but the physical property is noteworthy alongside a plant that appears in contexts of exile, restoration, and faithfulness under pressure.

4. **Embodied in a person.** Hadassah is the only individual in Scripture named after the myrtle. Her story — orphaned, concealed, faithful, willing to die, effective in intercession — gives the myrtle a narrative embodiment that the other six occurrences lack.

5. **Material for God's dwelling.** In {{ref:Neh.8.15}}, myrtle branches are among the materials for Sukkot booths. The booth (sukkah) is a temporary dwelling associated with God's sheltering presence (cf. {{ref:Isa.4.6}}, "a tabernacle for a shadow in the daytime"). Myrtle-material builds the structure of that covering.

6. **Location of God's presence.** In the Zechariah vision, the angel of the LORD does not stand on a mountain or in a temple — he stands among the myrtle trees. The myrtle grove is where God's representative stations himself while patrol agents report on the state of the earth.

7. **Everlasting.** The myrtle is an evergreen — it does not lose its leaves seasonally. {{ref:Isa.55.13}} calls the myrtle-replacement an "everlasting sign that shall not be cut off." The permanence of the plant mirrors the permanence of the promise.

---

## Connections

- **{{symbol:thorns}}** — Myrtle is the explicit anti-thorn in {{ref:Isa.55.13}}. Thorns choke the word ({{ref:Mat.13.22}}); myrtle replaces them.
- **{{symbol:shadow}}** — The myrtle trees in {{ref:Zec.1.8}} are in the H4699 *mᵉtsullâh* (shade), from H6751 *tsâlal*. This connects to the broader pattern of dwelling "under the shadow" ({{ref:Ps.91.1}}).
- **{{symbol:tree}}** — Myrtle is a specific type of tree. Trees throughout Scripture appear as images of people or nations ({{ref:Ezek.31.6}}). The myrtle is a particular kind: evergreen, low, fragrant.
- **{{symbol:fig-tree}}** — Vine and fig together represent covenant peace and shadow ({{ref:Mic.4.4}}). When the barren fig is cursed ({{ref:Mat.21.19}}), its shadow fails. The myrtle replaces the thorn/brier ({{ref:Isa.55.13}}) and provides material for the booth that gives true shadow ({{ref:Neh.8.15}}).
- **{{symbol:booths}}** — Myrtle branches build the Sukkot booth ({{ref:Neh.8.15}}). The booth is "a tabernacle for a shadow" ({{ref:Isa.4.6}}). Sukkot booths are themselves "a shadow of things to come" ({{ref:Col.2.17}}).
- **{{symbol:to-and-fro}}** — The agents in {{ref:Zec.1.10}}-{{ref:Zec.1.11}} walk "to and fro through the earth" while stationed among the myrtle trees. The myrtle grove is the base of operations for this patrol activity.

---

## Occurrences by Sense

### Restoration — replacement of the cursed order
- {{ref:Isa.55.13}} — myrtle replaces brier; everlasting sign not cut off
- {{ref:Isa.41.19}} — God plants myrtle in wilderness alongside cedar, shittah, oil tree

### God's hidden presence among His people
- {{ref:Zec.1.8}} — man on red horse stands among myrtle trees in the shade
- {{ref:Zec.1.10}} — man among myrtle trees explains the to-and-fro patrol agents
- {{ref:Zec.1.11}} — patrol agents report to the angel of the LORD standing among the myrtle trees

### Material for covenant dwelling
- {{ref:Neh.8.15}} — myrtle branches gathered for building Sukkot booths

### Personal embodiment
- {{ref:Est.2.7}} — Hadassah (= myrtle), that is Esther — orphan, beautiful, faithful, concealed

